  "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } & =\\frac{-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$",
